2.2 NEAREST NEIGHBOR GRAPHS AND UNWANTED CONNECTIVITY
Geometric machine learning approaches attempt to capture the underlying structure of M from noisy samples X , typically beginning with the construction of a nearest neighbor graph. These graphs use connectivity rules of two flavors: ε-radius, or k-nearest neighbor (k-NN) (Bernstein et al., 2001). The ε-radius connectivity scheme asserts that for any two vertices a and b, an edge exists between them if ∥a − b∥2 ≤ ε. The k-NN rule on the other hand asserts that the edge exists only if b is one of the k nearest neighbors of a, or vice versa. While the ε-radius rule is more amenable to theoretical analysis, the k-NN rule is used more often in practice.
